{"canonical_record":{"canonical_payload_sha256":"8f2763e9903863c8c80d7567a122740bcb28fca7b13e153120760d2a1bb077fd","full_review_record_id":"fullrev_37fba8ad723b038c430914c4f64f7abff73da2044edb5ee5de70fe5f2cc44738","overall_assessment":"The paper presents a novel combination of match-LSTM and Pointer Networks to address the SQuAD dataset, introducing two distinct model variants (sequence and boundary) that effectively handle variable-length answers. The work provides useful insights into model behavior through analyses of answer length and question type, and demonstrates competitive performance against feature-engineered baselines.","paper_id":"B1-q5Pqxl","paper_identity_sha256":"23a2d9706b62b639650f75ff571d2e8896f2adeba1e0d501cbdac541761bf10e","prompt_identity_sha256":"9c0b37ae8b2e374c11accdcddce9c5c8e48a7524d2bf4c82a9021b4d44c67333","raw_response_sha256":"3b3f62cef77b2e28b13609b0ddbeeb3b3025f22aca7f3cd48cfa2c350906abe0","request_identity_sha256":"ce5ef1f78e7f1a81f3c183fc415e6a9e982e260fddf7d5ceba16d018960a4755","reviewer_identity_sha256":"ffb7de3c92b60fd85ca4efbe762244cb204797e709b6c35d64f8aa04c356bd04","role":"ORIG","schema_version":"agentreview-full-review-corpus-v4","structural_context_sha256":"246e42ed15511d2829c5c387e87312a72aba34e11eccfcf439d0ef8721f75d25","subdimension_sections":[{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-003","SRC-032"],"review_text":"The paper does not introduce a new problem formulation or dataset; it utilizes the existing SQuAD dataset. However, it clearly motivates the specific challenge of SQuAD compared to prior datasets: the absence of candidate answers and the variable length of answers, which renders previous multiple-choice or single-token prediction methods unsuitable. This framing effectively highlights the specific properties of the task that necessitate the proposed architectural changes.","subdimension_id":"ORIG-PROB"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-006","SRC-010","SRC-011","SRC-018"],"review_text":"The methodology is not a single new algorithm but rather the application of two existing components (match-LSTM and Pointer Net) to a new task. The novelty lies in the specific architectural design of two variants: a sequence model that generates tokens sequentially with a stop condition, and a boundary model that predicts start and end indices. The boundary model is a distinct algorithmic choice that enforces the consecutivity of the answer span, which is a meaningful structural constraint for this task.","subdimension_id":"ORIG-MTH"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-028","SRC-029","SRC-030"],"review_text":"The paper provides original analyses that deepen understanding of the model's performance. It identifies that longer answers are significantly harder to predict, showing a drop in F1 and exact match scores for answers exceeding 9 tokens. It also categorizes performance by question type, noting that 'when' questions are easiest while 'why' questions are hardest due to answer diversity. Additionally, attention weight visualizations are used to demonstrate how the model aligns question words with passage tokens, providing insight into the matching mechanism.","subdimension_id":"ORIG-ANL"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-024","SRC-025"],"review_text":"The experimental setup relies on the existing SQuAD v1.1 dataset and standard evaluation metrics (Exact Match and F1). The paper does not propose a new benchmark, dataset, or evaluation protocol. The experimental contribution is limited to the application of the proposed models to this existing benchmark and the comparison against baselines provided by the dataset creators and other concurrent works.","subdimension_id":"ORIG-EXP"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-006","SRC-033"],"review_text":"The paper claims a creative combination of match-LSTM (for textual entailment) and Pointer Net (for sequence-to-sequence copying). The rationale is well-articulated: match-LSTM is used to match the question against the passage, while Pointer Net is used to constrain the output to tokens from the input, which is necessary because SQuAD answers are spans from the passage rather than from a fixed vocabulary. The paper explicitly contrasts this with prior work that used Pointer Networks for single-token copying, highlighting the novelty of using it for multi-token span extraction.","subdimension_id":"ORIG-COM"},{"paper_anchor_ids":[],"review_text":"The paper does not present negative results or a critical assessment of existing methods as a primary contribution. While it notes the limitations of its own model on 'why' questions and long answers, these are presented as areas for future work rather than as a systematic failure analysis or a negative result challenging broader assumptions in the field.","subdimension_id":"ORIG-NEG"}]},"disposition":"CANONICAL_FULL_REVIEW_COMMITTED","item_commit_sha256":"bbd62a6a810401c5964a6a469613007cf7050331d5735684b3a003b3e2e6cd28","paper_id":"B1-q5Pqxl","paper_ordinal":1,"raw_attempts":[{"assistant_content_base64":"","assistant_content_sha256":"3b3f62cef77b2e28b13609b0ddbeeb3b3025f22aca7f3cd48cfa2c350906abe0","attempt_index":0,"error":"","finish_reason":"stop","paper_id":"B1-q5Pqxl","raw_provider_response_base64":"","raw_provider_response_sha256":"d654ab8d9d223cdec4c020d27dcc753980781837a31b8a59190010bd3d7d0272","request_identity_sha256":"ce5ef1f78e7f1a81f3c183fc415e6a9e982e260fddf7d5ceba16d018960a4755","role":"ORIG","schema_version":"no-memory-full-review-corpus-build-v4","usage":{"completion_tokens":975,"prompt_tokens":19162,"prompt_tokens_details":null,"total_tokens":20137},"valid":true}],"request_identity_sha256":"ce5ef1f78e7f1a81f3c183fc415e6a9e982e260fddf7d5ceba16d018960a4755","role":"ORIG","role_ordinal":3,"schema_version":"corpus-build-bootstrap-checkpoint-v4"}